# R42 revision 9 — check 16, third leg: DEMONSTRATE that each control the other two legs
# claim actually discriminates. This leg exists because of the round-8 rejection. The
# auditor's finding F1 was that check 16's payloads printed the status of every critical
# command instead of asserting it, so nothing the decision called a failure control could
# make the script fail. Revision 9 converts every one of them into an assertion — and a
# control asserted to be capable of failing, without a run showing it fail, is exactly the
# defect that rejection names. So each control is shown failing here.
#
# METHOD. For every control, the owning leg is run once with exactly ONE input substituted
# (`PERTURB=<name>`), and the run must (a) exit non-zero and (b) print that control's own
# `ASSERT-FAIL <name>` line. Exit status alone is not discrimination — a rate-limited fetch
# would also exit non-zero — so the named line is required too, and any run whose output
# carries a 403/429/rate-limit marker is reported VOID rather than counted as a control
# that fired. Thirteen controls are demonstrated, seven in the first leg and six in the
# second. Each leg is also run CLEAN before and after, which both proves the unperturbed
# script passes and, by diffing the two clean runs, asserts the rerun rule each leg's log
# states instead of leaving it as a claim.
#
# WHAT THIS LEG FOUND AND THE OTHER LOGS RECORD. Section B0 asserts that all eight files
# check 16 reads are byte-identical at tags v1.22.0 and v1.23.0. Revision 8's one-file tree
# control therefore did not separate those two releases; it authenticates content, not a
# release number. The second leg now hashes all eight files and the perturbation used is
# v1.21.0, whose `args.rs` differs.
#
# Run once, 2026-09-14T23:03:07Z, which is 2026-09-14 16:03 America/Los_Angeles (the stamp
# is the output's mtime; the script prints no timestamp of its own). It drives nineteen
# executions of the other two legs, so it is the slowest artifact here.
#
# Working directory: the run directory. Scratch directory: <scratch>/r42r9/ctl, outside the
# repository; `LEG1` and `LEG2` name the two leg scripts, which default to `./check16.sh`
# and `./check16b.sh` in the current directory. Extract all three payloads from these three
# logs into one directory, `chmod +x` them, and this script drives the other two. Nothing is
# read from or written to the repository, so committing this revision cannot change what it
# does.
#
# RERUN RULE. On any host: every `ASSERT-OK` line and the final `CONTROLS RESULT: PASSED`
# must reproduce, as must the thirteen `ASSERT-FAIL` lines quoted from the perturbed runs
# and the `8 of 8` identity count in section B0. What varies is the three `INFO Done in`
# wall-clock timings printed inside section A's leg-1 diff: this leg gives both clean runs
# one scratch directory, so no path line appears in that diff. The assertion is that no
# differing line falls outside the rule, not that the diff is a fixed string.
#
# WHAT THIS LEG DOES NOT SHOW. That the perturbed inputs are the only ones that would fire
# each control — a control shown to discriminate on one substitution is not proved to
# discriminate on every possible one. And it demonstrates the scripts, not the subject:
# nothing here bears on what cargo-binstall verifies, which is the other two legs' business.
#
# ---8<--- check16-controls.sh ---8<---
